ction $y=g(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=1.5$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=1.5.$ Applying the above formula with $x=1.5$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(1.5) & ≈\\frac{g(1.5+1)-g(1.5)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(2.5)-g(1.5)}{1} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71921/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "419134c3-3177-5d7d-86f6-899a73338b1c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71921"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-47386"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/5f770f5be6b6131533dc23482281e82024737a4e5a61440dfe1baf5108786381.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=6.$  Applying the above formula with $x=6$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{g(6+4)-g(6)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(10)-g(6)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-47387/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "c3f31dd7-4596-5079-8606-deba3021c67e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-47387/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "74a7f940-a53d-55b1-8040-b17996cdd972" :answer/type :a